      "purpose": "Low-cost ($13), open-source 3D-printed lumbar spine phantom for simulation training in spinal and epidural anesthesia, validated against a commercial Simulab trainer by 22 staff anesthesiologists.",
      "description": "Neuraxial anesthesia (spinal and epidural) is a procedure with a significant learning curve traditionally taught at the bedside, exposing patients to avoidable risk. Commercial simulation phantoms cost hundreds to thousands of dollars and are rarely available outside academic centers. This phantom is built from CT scan DICOM data segmented using free software (ITK-SNAP), 3D-printed in PLA on a desktop printer ($13 material cost), and filled with an echogenic psyllium-gelatin solution to simulate tissue and enable ultrasound imaging. In a formal study, 22 staff anesthesiologists compared it to the Simulab commercial phantom: the open-source model showed superior fidelity for loss of resistance, dural puncture, and ultrasound imaging. All design files are freely available on GitHub. Published in PLOS ONE (2018). Reference: https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0191664",
