      "description": "Negative pressure wound therapy (vacuum-assisted closure, VAC) is a standard of care for complex wounds — including combat injuries, blast wounds, diabetic ulcers, and post-surgical dehiscence — but commercial systems (KCI ActiV.A.C., Smith & Nephew PICO) cost thousands of dollars per unit and are systematically absent in LMICs and field hospitals. This device uses a small vacuum pump, a pressure transducer, and an Arduino microcontroller with digital display (total cost ≤$75). It allows the clinician to set any therapeutic range of intermittent negative pressure (25–175 mmHg) with configurable on/off cycles and has two independent safety mechanisms to prevent over-pressure. Bench-validated on a wound phantom: correct pressure patterns confirmed, exudate drainage up to 1 L/h — within full clinical range. Full hardware description, BOM, circuit schematics, and firmware are provided open-source in the paper supplementary material. Published in Journal of Clinical Medicine (2022). Reference: https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm11185417",
